Metro Planning Office (MPO): Nancy Olson
The TIS is required to identify impacts of the development at build-out to the roadway network in
the southeastern region of our metropolitan area.This would include rebuilding and paving the
collectors to and from the arterials adjacent to this large development. Most of the roads in south
Cheyenne are narrow rural roads and will not handle increased volumes. It also will include
intersection widening to add turn lanes and signalizations to many roads feeding the arterial
networks. College Drive will need to be straightened to eliminate the sweeping curve and
widened. These are serious impacts, substantial mitigations at an enormous cost. (Planning Staff
Note: These concerns should be noted, but are in regard to the preliminary plat, rather than the
annexation.)

Police Department: Brian Kozak
This development will have a serious impact to public safety. The City must plan to increase
police officers to service the planned build out of the project. Additionally, the PD will need space
for a substation (in a neighborhood fire station) to better serve the residents of this development. I
believe the developer should be required to pay for this infrastructure. (Planning Staff Note:
These concerns should be noted, but are in regard to the preliminary plat, rather than the
annexation.)

Public Works: Nathan Beauheim
Based on the FY2016 Budget, the Public Works Department estimates that the budgets of the
Traffic and Street & Alley divisions will need to be increased by approximately $331,000 per year
(in 2016 dollars) to provide service to this development at full build-out. This does not include
possible costs relating to the City potentially accepting responsibility for snow plowing or other
maintenance activities on exterior roadways such as College or Ave. C. (Planning Staff Note:
These concerns should be noted, but are in regard to the preliminary plat, rather than the
annexation.)

